  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:3kdkPUmrc20",
    "purpose": "Demonstrates how to clean and season a brand-new carbon steel wok so that it cooks an egg without sticking on its first use, and explains why wok seasoning differs from seasoning a Western skillet.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Wok seasoning relies mainly on forming black oxide and on proper preheating at each use, rather than on building thick polymer layers, so a new wok can be non-stick right away.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "New carbon steel woks ship with machine oil to prevent rust; scrub it off with soap and a sponge on every surface until nothing feels greasy.",
      "Seasoning has two parts: heating the metal to form black oxide, and building polymerized oil layers.",
      "Western skillets heat evenly, so polymer layers build up; woks heat unevenly (hot bottom, cooler sides), and the uneven expansion makes polymers hard to keep.",
      "Deglazing with soy sauce, rice wine and other acidic ingredients also breaks down polymer layers in a wok, so wok seasoning works more on a per-use basis.",
      "On a gas burner, tilt and lift the wok to heat every surface until it turns from bluish to dull silver to black, about 10 minutes.",
      "On electric or induction stoves, use a portable butane burner (up to around 15,000 BTU/hr) or a torch head to bring the flame to the wok.",
      "After heating, turn off the heat, rub oil over every exposed metal surface, then wipe it out with a clean paper towel to leave the thinnest layer.",
      "Stir-fries and deep frying are good first uses; with proper preheating, even eggs slide around in a freshly seasoned wok."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Cleaning a new wok",
        "summary": "Introduces the brand-new 14-inch flat-bottom carbon steel wok and shows how to scrub off the protective machine oil with soap and a sponge.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Understanding wok seasoning",
        "summary": "Explains seasoning as black oxide formation plus polymer build-up, and why uneven heating and acidic deglazing mean woks don't hold thick polymer layers the way Western skillets do.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Heating techniques for woks",
        "summary": "Shows tilting the wok over a gas flame to heat every surface, and offers alternatives for electric or induction cooks: a portable butane burner, an outdoor burner, or a torch head.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Applying the first layer",
        "summary": "Continues heating the wok dry until every surface changes color and forms black oxide, which takes about 10 minutes.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Finalizing the seasoning",
        "summary": "With the heat off, rubs oil over every exposed metal surface, then wipes it out with a clean paper towel to leave a very thin layer.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Testing the wok with eggs",
        "summary": "Suggests stir-fries or deep frying as first uses, then preheats the wok and scrambles eggs, which slide around without sticking.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 7
      }
    ],
